## 1. Introduction
The gustatory sense is a specialized chemosensory system dedicated to the translation of tastant identity and concentration into patterns of activity by separate populations of selectively tuned taste receptor cells (TRCs). TRCs are organized into taste buds on the tongue with ovoid structures typically composed of 50–100 cells that respond to five basic modalities: sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami (Scott, 2005). Sweet and umami compounds are detected by T1R heterodimers. Bitter compounds activate T2R receptors. Salt is sensed by the sodium channel ENaC while sour-sensing cells are defined by the expression of PKD2L1. TRCs contain multiple sites of highly variable structures for the accommodation of different chemical stimuli. The concentration of a particular tastant is sensed through the affinity level of a receptor, and the degree of activation of multiple receptors (Yarmolinsky et al., 2009).
However, to describe the taste objectively by a human tongue is almost impossible. And yet, the grading and quantification of taste are indispensable to product enhancement and quality control in food, beverage, and pharmaceutical industries (Toko, 1998; Hayashi et al., 2007; Uchida et al., 2003). Consequently, highly sensitive and high quality sensing systems were developed through: (1) the use of multichannel electronic tongues (i.e., sensor fusion technique), and (2) the consideration of broaderrange signals obtained through other sensing devices by principal component analysis (PCA) (i.e., global selectivity) (Katsube et al., 2005). However, the electronic tongue itself has several drawbacks (Toko and Habara, 2005): (1) its detecting part is made of seven–eight bulky glass electrodes of different lipid/polymer hybrid membranes filled with KCl aqueous solution, (2) the preconditioning procedures of its hybrid membrane may cause surface modifications on the membrane, (3) full characterization of its membrane is unavailable, and (4) the electrical potential of its membrane surface may vary by non-electrolytes such as sucrose.
However, the relatively large sizes of tastant molecules such as sucrose (Kaszuba et al., 2008) of $0.9\mathrm{-}0.98~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were difficult to be included into the voids of $11.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\times23.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ (Lee et al., 2011) (Fig. 1a) or the ones of $6.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\times6.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in the MOF-76 microcrystals (Rosi et al., 2005) (Fig. 1b). To enhance the differentiation of chemical stimuli, polyacrylic acid (PAA) is chosen to mimic TRCs for their variable structural functions. PAA is grafted on $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals to form PAA chelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals. Therefore, the conformational changes in the PAA macromolecules are thought to be related directly to the frequency and the magnitude of acid-base and metallic ion (i.e., $-\mathrm{COO-In-OOC-};$ crosslinking interactions on the $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystal surface, and also to the interactions among the proton-donating pendent –COOH groups of PAA and the various functional groups of –OH, –COOH, $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ , $\scriptstyle{C=0}$ and ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions of sucrose, caffeine, citric acid, sodium chloride, and monosodium glutamate in the test solutions (Sugama et al., 1984; Wis´niewska et al., 2009). The number and the frequency of – COO–In–OOC– chelated interactions will definitely interfere with the regular ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) happening in the $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ framework. Consequently, the overall PL responses will be altered. The concept of brushing a solvothermal processed $[\mathrm{In(OH)(bdc)}]_{n}/\mathrm{PAA}$ hybrid film on a substrate (Centrone et al., 2010) will be developed as well, so that $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals may be securely mounted onto a future device ready for its implementation in an aqueous medium.
## 2. Materials and methods
Chemicals, solvents and instrumentations are listed in the Supporting Information section.
## 2.1. Experimental procedures
## 2.1.1. Synthesis of $I I n(O H)(b d c)J_{n}$ microcrystals
$78{\mathrm{~mg}}$ $\mathrm{In}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $\left(0.26\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ and $30.0\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{d}\mathsf{c}$ $\left(0.18\mathrm{mmol}\right)^{\cdot}$ ) were co-dissolved in $8~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial (i.e., precursor solution). The precursor solution was heated to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in an oil bath for $10\mathrm{h}$ and the crystals generated were produced by cooling the solution to room temperature, isolating and rinsing with DMF. The crystals were then air dried for a day and characterized by FTIR, TGA, PXRD, PL, and SEM (Figs. S2–S6).
## 2.1.2. Taste-sensing of PAA chelated $I I n(O H)(b d c)J_{n}$ microcrystals
$10\mathrm{mg}$ of air dried $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals were immersed in $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of $10^{-2}\mathbf{M}$ aqueous solution of tastant codissolved with $4\:\mathrm{mg}$ of PAA in a $7\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The microcrystals were filtered, dried at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and characterized by PL. The experiments were repeated three times.
## 2.1.3. Tea-sensing of PAA chelated $I I n(O H)(b d c)J_{n}$ microcrystals
Tea solution: about $2g$ tea bag was soaked in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of water in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ scintillation vial. $10\mathrm{mg}$ of air dried $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals were immersed in $2\mathrm{mL}$ of tea solution co-dissolved with $4\:\mathrm{mg}$ of PAA in a $7\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The microcrystals were filtered, dried at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and characterized by PL.
## 2.1.4. Synthesis of MOF-76 microcrystals
2.1.5. Taste-sensing of MOF-76 microcrystals and the calibration curves of PL intensity vs. concentration
$10\mathrm{mg}$ of $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ vacuum oven dried MOF-76 microcrystals were immersed in $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $10^{-1}$ , $10^{-2}$ , $10^{-3}$ , $10^{-4}$ , and $10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solutions of tastant in a $7\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial individually for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The microcrystals were filtered, dried at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and characterized by PL. The experiments were repeated three times.
## 2.1.6. Synthesis and taste-sensing of $(C a_{3}(b t c)_{2}(D M F)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}J\cdot3H_{2}O$ microcrystals
21 mg $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c}$ $\mathrm{\langle0.1~mmol}$ ), $70.8\ \mathrm{mg}\ C a(N O_{3})_{2}\cdot4H_{2}O$ $(0.3\mathrm{mmol})\mathrm{\Omega}.$ , 1 mL $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $5\mathrm{mL}$ DMF were dissolved in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial to form a homogeneous solution at $\mathsf{p H}3.5$ as adjusted by a $0.4\mathrm{M}$ formic acid solution. The homogeneous solution was heated to $90^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in an oil bath for 2 days and the crystals were filtered, rinsed with DMF, and oven dried at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . $10\mathrm{mg}$ of dried $\mathsf{I C a}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ microcrystals were immersed in $2{\mathrm{mL}}$ of $10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solution of tastant for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The microcrystals were filtered, dried at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and characterized by PL.
## 2.1.7. Synthesis of $I I n(O H)(b d c)J_{n}$ microcrystals/PAA film
Into the synthetic precursor solution of $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n},$ 2 wt% of PAA with respect to DMF was added. The resulting solution was heated to $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an oil bath for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ . When the reactive solution became turbid, it was brushed onto a glass slide uniformly to form a film and allowed to be air dried.
## 2.1.8. Taste-sensing of $I I n(O H)(b d c)J_{n}$ microcrystals/PAA film
The film was immersed in $2\mathrm{mL}$ of $10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solution of tastant in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature, dried in a vacuum oven at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and characterized by PL.
## 2.1.9. Solutions preparation for UV/vis spectroscopy
PAA solutions at various $\mathsf{p H}$ values for PAA conformational studies: $6\mathrm{mg}$ of PAA were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of RO water in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial at pH values of 4 and 10 as adjusted by 0.03 M HCl (aq) and $0.03\mathrm{M}\mathrm{NaOH}$ (aq).
$1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solutions of sucrose, sodium chloride and monosodium glutamate were prepared.
$6\:\mathrm{mg}$ of PAA were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ sucrose or sodium chloride or monosodium glutamate solution to form $1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ tastant solution PAA, in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial.
$5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ caffeine aqueous solution and $1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ of citric acid aqueous solution were prepared for understanding bathochromatic shift in PL spectra of tastant-adsorbed $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals.
## 3. Results and discussion
Schematic representations of those frameworks had already been described elsewhere (Lee et al., 2011; Rosi et al., 2005). As shown in Figs. 1a and S1a, each $\mathrm{{In}(I I I)}$ atom in the guest-free, porous $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ framework has a coordination number of six. It is bound to four carboxylate oxygen atoms from four bdc linkers and two other bridging oxygen atoms from two $\upmu{-}\mathsf{O H}$ groups. Therefore, the framework is constructed from infinite chains of $\cdot\cdot\cdot0\mathrm{H-In-OH-In-OH\cdot\cdot}\ .$ connected by carboxylate linkers and possessed of $11.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\times23.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ nanochannels along the $c$ -axis (Lee et al., 2011). SEM image (Fig. S6) revealed that the as-synthesized $[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{OH})\mathrm{bdc}]_{n}$ microcrystal is a hexagonal rod.
As depicted by Figs. 1b and S1b, each $\mathrm{Tb(III)}$ center in MOF-76 has a coordination number of seven, and each btc possesses three carboxylate groups. Each Tb(III) atom is linked to a neighboring Tb(III) atom by: (1) three $-0\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}\mathrm{-}$ of the same carboxyl group from three different btc’s, and (2) three –O–C–benzene–C–O– of another three btc’s, and each Tb(III) atom is also bound to one terminal water ligand. The distance between any two Tb(III) atoms is $4.637\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in this infinite chain of Tb–O–C units. The chain is a $\scriptstyle4_{1}$ helix. Every four Tb(III) atoms on the chain will form one turn. Each $\scriptstyle4_{1}$ helix is connected to four neighboring $\scriptstyle4_{1}$ helices through the benzene ring of the btc link resulting in ${\bar{6}}.6{\bar{\mathsf{A}}}\times6.6{\bar{\mathsf{A}}}$ nanochannels in the $c$ -direction (Rosi et al., 2005). The tetragonal rod-like MOF-76 microcrystal with pores is observed by SEM (Fig. S10).
The FTIR spectra of PAA-chelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals in Fig. S13 showed that PAA macromolecules are successfully grafted onto $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals as indicated by the relatively increase of intensity in the IR absorption frequencies for $\scriptstyle-C=0$ stretch at $1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and for –OH stretch overtones at $2700{-}2500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Sugama et al.,1984; Colthup et al., 1990). The overlap of the TGA scans of water-adsorbed, and PAA-chelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals in Fig. S14a and c before a complete dehydration of water-adsorbed $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals around $250^{\circ}\mathsf C$ suggests that the $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ framework is not loaded with PAA macromolecules. The small-pore apertures of $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ framework are not suitable for the inclusion of PAA (Deng et al., 2012). Therefore, PAA can only be chelated and coated on the surfaces of the rod-like $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals as illustrated by the POM images in Fig. S12a and b. This is supported by the PXRD patterns in Fig. S15a and c showing that although the lattice framework of guest free, porous $[\mathrm{In}({\mathsf{O H}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ (Fig.S15a) is affected significantly by the inclusion of water (Fig. S15c), it does not change too much afterwards with the chelation of PAA (Fig. S15b and c).
Generally, the conformational changes of PAA are associated to the ionization of its carboxylic acid groups (i.e., –COOH) (Akkahat et al., 2012; Sarkar and Somasundaran, 2004). At $\mathsf{p H}=9.5$ , all of the –COOH groups should be deprotonated to the form of carboxylate groups (i.e. $-\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ . These negatively charged ions will cause electrostatic repulsion within the PAA chain and among adjacent chains, causing the polymer chains to become more of a stretching conformation (Nilsson et al., 2002). In contrast, when the pH value is lowered to $\sim6.5–4.5$ , the –COOH groups are proportionally less deprotonated and become more of a coiling conformation. Since the stretched and coiled conformations of PAA can be induced by pH variations in the aqueous medium, the hypsochromatic shift and absorbance enhancement for the UV–vis spectra of PAA aqueous solution at $\mathsf{p H}=4$ and $\mathsf{p H}=10$ in Fig. S17a are thought to be due to electrostatic repulsion forces among side chains, hydrogen bonding, and hydrophobic assembly between nearby side chains of PAA in water (Kim et al., 1999; McCullough et al., 1997).
Since the similar hypsochromatic shift and absorbance enhancement for the UV–vis spectra of PAA in aqueous solutions of sucrose, sodium chloride and monosodium glutamate were observed in Fig. S17b, we speculate that those three tastants can also induce the conformational changes of PAA at different levels (Fig. S17c) which will in turn influence the PL responses of PAAchelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals (Fig. 2(i), (iv) and (v)). On the other hand, caffeine-adsorbed and citric acid-adsorbed $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals can give distinct PL emission responses even without the use of PAA (Fig. 2(ii) and (iii)). We believe that the light absorbance properties of caffeine and citric acid as shown in Fig. S17d can interfere with the inherent PL emission properties of $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals. However, the distinct PL emission response of the basic tastant through PAAchelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals will not be interfered by other unknown ingredients. For example, caffeine with a PL emission signature at $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}{=}436\mathrm{nm}$ is consistently observed throughout the three different kinds of tea leaves of oolong, pu-erh, and green tea as shown in Fig. S18 (Aucamp et al., 2000). In addition to the recognition of tastants such as sucrose, caffeine, citric acid, sodium chloride and monosodium glutamate by PAA-chelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathsf{O H}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals, the quantification of the strength of those tastants is equally important. Fig. 3 shows the PL spectra of MOF-76 microcrystals immersed in different concentrations of aqueous solutions of five common tastants.
A film of supersaturation is created which induces nucleation and crystal growth of the free tastant near the surface of MOF-76 forming a dark spiky coating as shown by the POM images in Fig. S12i–m. The TGA weight loss profiles of all kinds of tastantadsorbed MOF-76’s (Fig. S19b–d and f) and water-adsorbed MOF (Fig. $\mathsf{S19g}\cdot$ are more or less similar, but except for the one of sodium chloride-adsorbed MOF (Fig. S19e) because the ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ ions are small enough to be included in MOF-76. PXRD patterns in Fig. S20b–f of the different kinds of tastant-adsorbed MOF-76 microcrystals also show that the lattice framework of MOF-76 is not altered significantly by the adsorption of different tastants and the inclusion of sodium chloride.
The tastant-adsorbed MOF-76 microcrystals are excited at $300\mathrm{nm}$ and monitored at $553\mathrm{nm}$ attributed to ${^5\mathrm{D}}_{4}\ensuremath{\rightarrow}{^7}\mathrm{F}_{5}$ energy transitions of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion. However, if the –OH vibrational group from water is present during this energy transfer, the luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion will be reduced. Since the –OH, $-C=0$ or ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ groups of tastants will interact with the –OH group of water included in MOF-76 through hydrogen bonding, the $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bond stretching of the incorporated water will be limited, and thus, allow enhancement of the PL intensity of the tastant adsorbed MOF-76 solids (Chen et al., 2008). Therefore, the PL intensity activated from the tastant solution increases in the following descending order in solution concentrations: $10^{-1}>\bar{10}^{-2}>10^{-3}>\bar{10}^{-4}>10^{-5}\mathrm{~M}.$ .
This trend is observed in Fig. 3 and the PL emission intensity values monitored at $553\mathrm{nm}$ , $\varphi$ , of the tastant-adsorbed MOF-76 solids are plotted against the logarithmic concentrations of the tastant, log C, in Fig. 4. The calculated least square lines for sucrose, caffeine, citric acid, sodium chloride and monosodium glutamate are listed in Eqs. (1)–(5), respectively:
$$
{\begin{array}{r l}&{\varphi=96.1\log C+1084.5}\\ &{\varphi=126.9\log C+1047.7}\\ &{\varphi=70.4\log C+763.4}\\ &{\varphi=40.5\log C+616.9}\\ &{\varphi=140.8\log C+1132}\end{array}}
$$
Noticeably, these relations agree with the well-known WeberFechner law of human sensing evaluation that sensation is proportional to the logarithm of the stimulus intensity (Pfaffmann, 1959). On the other hand, the $\tau$ scales for the four basic tastes, sweet, bitter, sour, and salty (i.e., none for umami) are given in terms of concentrations, C, (i.e., gram per $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ ) in Eqs. (6)–(9) respectively (Indow, 1969):
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\tau=2.65\log(C/0.48)}\\ {\tau=2.35\log(C/0.00011)}\\ {\tau=2.27\log(C/0.0010)}\\ {\tau=2.83\log(C/0.056)}\end{array}
$$
The significance of the $\tau$ scale is that the same $\tau$ value of different tastes represents the same strength (Indow, 1969). By combining the two sets of equations of Eqs. (1)–(4) with Eqs. (6)–(9), the $\tau$ scales can be related to the PL emission intensity at $553\mathrm{nm}$ for sucrose, caffeine, citric acid and sodium chloride, and give Eqs. (10)–(13) respectively:
$$
\tau=0.0276\varphi-19.69
$$
$$
\tau=0.0699\varphi-31.31
$$
Lastly, PAA is chosen not only to mimic TRCs for their variable structural functions and be chelated onto $[\mathrm{In}({\mathsf{O H}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ solid surfaces, but also to serve as a binder to form a continuous $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}/\mathrm{PAA}$ hybrid film (Lee et al., 2001). The advantages of this approach are: (1) $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals can be brushed easily on any surfaces to form a transparent thin film (Fig. 6a and b), (2) the needle-like $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ microcrystals can be spread evenly as a dispersant in the PAA matrix and mounted securely on a substrate for future device implementation (Fig. 6c) and (3) the taste-discriminating capabilities based on PL emission responses still remained functional (Fig. 6d).
## 4. Conclusions
Current studies had clearly illustrated that our MOF-based ‘‘biomimetic tongue’’ is capable of distinguishing the five basic tastes of sweet, bitter, sour, salty and umami derived from the five corresponding tastants of sucrose, caffeine, citric acid, sodium chloride and monosodium glutamate. It is because: (1) PAA is used to mimic TRCs for their variable structural functions enabling the PAA chelated $[\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{OH}})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}$ solids to emit a PL response for a particular tastant (Fig. 2), (2) there is a linear relationship between the PL emission intensity at $553\mathrm{nm}$ , $\varphi$ , and the logarithmic concentration, $\log C,$ , of a tastant (Fig. 4), (3) the dependence of $\tau$ scale on $\varphi$ is established to quantify the strength of a taste, and (4) the 3D PCA contour map depending of the PL properties PC1, PC2 and PC3, is applied for taste identification (Fig. 5). Other macromolecules and other tastants such as quinine sulfate, amino acids or beer, will be used to establish a more global taste space (Toko, 1998). The approach of making a $[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{bdc})]_{n}/\mathrm{PAA}$ hybrid film may also be integrated with membrane and textile technology (Medeiros et al., 2009; Zhang et al., 2009) in our future studies.
